# MASTER DEVELOPMENT PROMPT: MIND EXPEDITION – LIVE QUIZ COMPETITION PLATFORM

Develop a professional, interactive and visually engaging live quiz competition platform named **MIND EXPEDITION**.

The platform should provide the experience of a professionally conducted televised quiz competition, with a quizmaster presenting questions, participants or teams seated in front of a large display, audience participation and real-time score updates.

The system must be flexible enough to conduct multiple quiz competitions, with different participants, question banks, numbers of rounds, round formats, time limits and scoring systems.

The platform must not be restricted to the five rounds described below. The administrator must be able to create additional rounds, select the type and number of rounds, configure their rules and scoring, and arrange them in any desired sequence.

Do not create a static demonstration or a simple quiz website. Develop a functional live quiz management system with synchronized real-time interactions.

# PART 1: OVERALL PLATFORM ARCHITECTURE

Create three separate, interconnected interfaces.

## A. Administrator Panel

The administrator should be able to:

- Create multiple independent quiz competitions.
- Edit, duplicate, schedule, activate, complete or archive quizzes.
- Add or remove participants and teams.
- Create different rounds for different competitions.
- Configure question banks.
- Set scoring systems and time limits.
- Upload images, audio, videos and other question-related media.
- Configure buzzer functionality.
- Monitor live scores and competition progress.
- View the final results and download reports.

Every quiz must have its own independent question bank, participant records, round settings, scores and results.

Changes made to one quiz must not affect another quiz.

## B. Quizmaster Console

Create a dedicated interface for the quizmaster.

The quizmaster should be able to:

- Start and end the competition.
- Select the active round.
- Display the next question on the main screen.
- Start, pause, resume and reset the timer.
- Display questions one at a time.
- Reveal answers when required.
- Mark answers as correct, incorrect or unanswered.
- Award or deduct marks.
- Activate and deactivate the buzzer.
- View the order in which participants press the buzzer.
- Select the next participant to answer when applicable.
- Move to the next question or round.
- View live scores and rankings.
- Make authorized manual score corrections with a recorded reason.
- Pause the entire competition in case of technical problems.

The quizmaster must have full control over the competition. No question or answer should advance automatically unless the quizmaster has enabled that option.

## C. Main Display / Projector Interface

This interface will be displayed on a large screen or projector in front of the participants and audience.

Design it for a 16:9 display, preferably 1920 × 1080 resolution, with responsive scaling.

Display the following information:

- MIND EXPEDITION title and branding.
- Competition name.
- Current round name and number.
- Current question.
- Participant or team name.
- Countdown timer.
- Current scores.
- Buzzer results and participant queue.
- Round completion status.
- Leaderboard.
- Final winner announcement.

Use large, readable typography and high-contrast colours.

The display should have attractive transitions, animations and sound effects appropriate for a professional quiz competition.

The audience screen must be read-only. It must not display administrative controls, answer keys or confidential question-bank information.

# PART 2: QUIZ CREATION AND ROUND CONFIGURATION

## 1. Create multiple quizzes

Provide a **Create New Quiz** button.

When the administrator clicks it, allow the following details to be entered:

- Quiz title.
- Quiz description.
- Competition date.
- Number of participants or teams.
- Individual or team-based competition.
- Names of participants or teams.
- Number of rounds.
- Round sequence.
- Maximum marks.
- Quizmaster name.
- Competition status.

Examples of separate quizzes:

- MIND EXPEDITION – Pharmaceutics
- MIND EXPEDITION – General Pharmacy
- MIND EXPEDITION – Pharmaceutical Sciences
- MIND EXPEDITION – Intercollegiate Quiz Competition
- MIND EXPEDITION – Annual College Quiz

Every quiz must function independently.

## 2. Flexible round builder

This is one of the most important platform requirements.

Do not hard-code the five rounds given below.

Create a flexible **Round Builder** in the administrator panel.

The administrator must be able to:

- Add as many rounds as required.
- Select the type of each round.
- Change the order of rounds.
- Rename rounds.
- Set the number of questions.
- Set the time limit.
- Assign marks for correct answers.
- Assign negative marks for incorrect answers.
- Decide marks for unanswered questions.
- Enable or disable a round.
- Add bonus questions.
- Decide whether a round is individual or team-based.
- Choose whether questions are presented horizontally or consecutively.
- Configure whether the participant answers verbally or through the mobile interface.

Each round must have its own independent settings.

The administrator should be able to save a round configuration as a reusable template for future competitions.

**Important:** The maximum marks of a quiz must be calculated automatically based on the configured rounds, number of questions and scoring rules. Do not use a fixed maximum score.

# PART 3: DEFAULT FIVE ROUNDS OF MIND EXPEDITION

Create the following five rounds as the default round templates.

The administrator must be able to modify all their parameters before a competition starts.

## ROUND I – BASIC ROUND

<text color="secondary" weight="medium">Default maximum: 50 marks | Time: 30 seconds per question</text>

**Format:**

- Five questions will be asked to each participant or team.
- Questions will be asked horizontally, one at a time to each team.
- For example, Question 1 will be asked to Team A, then Team B, then Team C, and so on.
- After all teams have answered Question 1, Question 2 will begin.
- The quizmaster controls the question sequence.

**Scoring:**

- Correct answer: +10 marks.
- Incorrect answer: 0 marks.
- Unanswered question: 0 marks.
- No negative marking.

**Maximum marks:** 5 × 10 = 50 marks per participant or team.

**Quizmaster controls:**

- Display question.
- Start 30-second timer.
- Record the answer as correct or incorrect.
- Move to the next participant.
- Move to the next question.

## ROUND II – RAPID FIRE ROUND

<text color="secondary" weight="medium">Default maximum: 50 marks | Total time: 90 seconds per participant or team</text>

**Format:**

- Five questions will be asked to each participant or team.
- Questions should have one-word or two-word answers.
- The participant must answer as many questions as possible within 1.5 minutes.
- Questions should appear one after another.
- The quizmaster should have the ability to skip a question and move to the next one.
- The rapid-fire timer should continue running while questions are being asked, unless the quizmaster pauses it.

**Scoring:**

- Correct answer: +10 marks.
- Incorrect answer: 0 marks.
- Pass or unanswered question: 0 marks.
- No negative marking.

**Maximum marks:** 50 marks.

**Additional controls:**

- Display a prominent countdown timer.
- Show the number of questions attempted.
- Allow the quizmaster to accept short answers.
- Allow the quizmaster to mark answers as correct or incorrect.
- Automatically move to the next question when the quizmaster confirms the answer or selects Skip.

## ROUND III – STRAIGHTFORWARD ROUND

<text color="secondary" weight="medium">Default maximum: 50 marks | Time: 30 seconds per question</text>

**Format:**

- Five questions will be asked to each participant or team.
- The default mode should allow the quizmaster to ask all five questions to one participant consecutively before moving to the next participant.
- Provide an alternative setting to present questions horizontally, one at a time to each team.
- Questions should be straightforward and based on subject knowledge.
- All questions should have a 30-second time limit.

**Scoring:**

- Correct answer: +10 marks.
- Incorrect answer: 0 marks.
- Unanswered question: 0 marks.
- No negative marking.

**Maximum marks:** 50 marks.

The quizmaster should have manual answer evaluation controls.

## ROUND IV – VISUAL ROUND

<text color="secondary" weight="medium">Default maximum: 40 marks | Time: 30 seconds per question</text>

**Format:**

- Four questions will be asked to each participant or team.
- Questions will be asked horizontally, one at a time to each team.
- Each question must contain an image, photograph, diagram, illustration, graph or other relevant visual material.
- The participant must identify the object, interpret the image or answer a question related to the visual.

**Possible visual question categories:**

- Identify a pharmaceutical instrument.
- Identify a dosage form.
- Identify a scientist or important personality.
- Identify a medicinal plant.
- Identify a chemical structure.
- Identify a laboratory apparatus.
- Interpret a pharmaceutical diagram.
- Identify a drug delivery system.
- Identify a medical device.
- Interpret a graph or experimental result.

**Scoring:**

- Correct answer: +10 marks.
- Incorrect answer: -5 marks.
- Unanswered question: 0 marks.

**Maximum possible marks:** 40 marks.

**Quizmaster controls:**

- Upload or select an image.
- Display the image on the projector.
- Start the 30-second timer.
- Accept the participant's verbal answer.
- Award or deduct marks.
- Move to the next team.

Ensure that uploaded images are displayed clearly and proportionally without distortion.

## ROUND V – CURRENT AFFAIRS IN PHARMACEUTICAL SCIENCES

<text color="secondary" weight="medium">Default maximum: 40 marks | Time: 30 seconds per question</text>

**Format:**

- Four questions will be asked to each participant or team.
- Questions will be asked horizontally, one at a time to each participant.
- Questions should focus on recent developments and important events related to pharmaceutical sciences.

**Suggested subject areas:**

- Recent pharmaceutical discoveries.
- New drug approvals.
- Drug regulatory developments.
- CDSCO and international regulatory developments.
- Pharmaceutical industry updates.
- Drug delivery innovations.
- Biotechnology and pharmaceutical research.
- Pharmacovigilance.
- Public health developments.
- WHO initiatives.
- Important pharmaceutical conferences and scientific achievements.
- Recent developments in clinical research.

**Scoring:**

- Correct answer: +10 marks.
- Incorrect answer: -5 marks.
- Unanswered question: 0 marks.

**Maximum possible marks:** 40 marks.

**Question management:**

- Allow the administrator to enter current-affairs questions manually.
- Permit importing questions from an Excel or CSV file.
- Optionally provide AI-assisted question generation from verified current-affairs material.
- For AI-generated current-affairs questions, store the source URL and publication date for administrator verification.
- Do not automatically add unverified or outdated information to a live quiz.
- Allow the quizmaster to reveal the correct answer after the response.

# PART 4: ADDITIONAL OPTIONAL QUIZ ROUNDS

Provide an expandable library of additional rounds.

The administrator should be able to select any combination of rounds, use the same type multiple times with different questions, or create a completely new round.

The following formats should be available:

## 1. BUZZER ROUND – FASTEST FINGER FIRST

<badge color="success">Essential feature</badge>

This is a highly important round and must be implemented with particular attention to real-time synchronization and response fairness.

### Basic mechanism

Each participant or team must have access to a unique mobile interface through their smartphone.

Participants will be seated in front of the main display.

When the quizmaster displays a question and activates the buzzer, all registered participants will see a prominent button on their mobile screens labelled:

**BUZZ IN!**

When a participant presses this button, the system must immediately register the response.

### Buzzer operation

1. The quizmaster displays the question on the main screen.
2. The quizmaster clicks **Activate Buzzer**.
3. All participants' mobile buzzer buttons become active.
4. Participants press the button as quickly as possible.
5. The server records each accepted press using an authoritative server timestamp and a unique sequence number.
6. The participant who presses first receives the first opportunity to answer.
7. The main display prominently shows the first participant's name or team name.
8. The main display also shows the names of all other participants who pressed the button, arranged in their order of response.
9. The quizmaster can view the complete response queue.
10. The quizmaster invites the first participant to answer.

**Example of main-screen display:**

